<meta type=article>
  <title | Linear Regression: A Brief Introduction>
  <author | Ariel Balter>
</meta>

<section | Introduction>
Linear regression is a method for predicting a continuous outcome <$ y $> from
one or more predictors <$ x $>.<note | The term "regression" was coined by Galton (1886) in his study 
of parent-child height relationships.> It is among the most widely used statistical
techniques, with applications in economics, biology, engineering, and data
science. The key assumption is that the relationship between the predictor and
the outcome is approximately linear.

<section | The Model>
The basic linear model relates a single predictor to an outcome as:

<$$ #eqn:model | y = \beta_0 + \beta_1 x + \varepsilon $$>

where <$ \beta_0 $> is the intercept (the expected value of <$ y $> when <$ x = 0 $>),
<$ \beta_1 $> is the slope (the expected change in <$ y $> per unit increase in <$ x $>),
and <$ \varepsilon $><note placement=side | The error term is typically assumed i.i.d. with 
mean zero: <$ \varepsilon \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2) $>.> is the error term capturing 
unexplained variation. The equivalent
in Python notation is:

```python
y = beta_0 + beta_1 * x + epsilon
```

<section | Least Squares Estimation>
The ordinary least squares criterion minimizes residuals relative to the model
defined in <ref #eqn:model>. It chooses the parameter values that minimize
the sum of squared residuals:

<$$ \text{minimize} \sum_{i=1}^{n} (y_i - \beta_0 - \beta_1 x_i)^2 $$>

Squaring the residuals penalizes large deviations more heavily and yields a
differentiable objective. We can define the loss function in Python as:

```python
def squared_error(y_true, y_pred):
    return ((y_true - y_pred) ** 2).sum()
```

<section | Closed-Form Solutions>
Differentiating the objective and setting the derivatives to zero yields
analytic expressions for the optimal parameter estimates. The slope estimate is:

<$$ \hat{\beta}_1 = \frac{\sum (x_i - \bar{x})(y_i - \bar{y})}{\sum (x_i - \bar{x})^2} $$>

and the intercept estimate is:

<$$ \hat{\beta}_0 = \bar{y} - \hat{\beta}_1 \bar{x} $$>

Here <$ \bar{x} $> and <$ \bar{y} $> are the sample means of the predictor and outcome,
respectively. In practice, a library is used:

<``` python #code:scikit | from sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression
model = LinearRegression().fit(X, y)
slope, intercept = model.coef_[0], model.intercept_ ```>

<section | Goodness of Fit>
The coefficient of determination <$ R^2 $> measures the proportion of variance in
the outcome explained by the model:<note placement=foot | <$ R^2 $> can be negative when the model 
fits worse than a horizontal line, e.g., when evaluated on a held-out test set.>

<$$ #eqn:r2 | R^2 = 1 - \frac{\text{SS}_{\text{res}}}{\text{SS}_{\text{tot}}} $$>

where <$ \text{SS}_{\text{res}} = \sum (y_i - \hat{y}_i)^2 $> is the residual sum of
squares and <$ \text{SS}_{\text{tot}} = \sum (y_i - \bar{y})^2 $> is the total sum of
squares. An <$ R^2 $> near 1 indicates a good fit; near 0 indicates the model
explains little of the observed variation. To compute it:

```python
from sklearn.metrics import r2_score
score = r2_score(y_true, y_pred)
```

<figure #fig:scatter src="assets/scatter-regression.svg" | A scatter plot of predictor <$ x $> versus outcome <$ y $>, with the OLS regression line overlaid.>

See <ref #fig:scatter> for a visual illustration. The slope of the regression
line is the estimate from <ref #eqn:model>, and goodness of fit is reported as
<ref #eqn:r2>. A reference to an undefined target would look like <ref #eqn:missing> in
the rendered output.
